<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fruto Chamorro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1804–1855</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fruto Chamorro was a Nicaraguan Conservative politician from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> who became supreme director of Nicaragua in 1853 and, under the constitution of 1854, its first president. His rule provoked a Liberal revolt from León in 1854, which drew in the American filibuster <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a>. He died of illness during the civil war in March 1855, and his family remained prominent in Nicaraguan politics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fruto-chamorro.jpg" alt="Fruto Chamorro" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucho84t · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:01_Fruto_Chamorro_P%C3%A9rez_1854-1855.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
